    "body": "By Charlotte Goldstone\n20 November 2026\nThe anticipated recovery in global airfreight capacity next year may prove less substantial than headline growth forecasts suggest.\nTwo aviation consultancies are warning that much of the increase will constitute restoration of capacity lost during the Gulf conflict, rather than new supply.\nAevean forecasts international capacity growth of 6% to 7% next year, against demand growth of 5%-6%; Rotate expects capacity to increase 5% to -6%, compared with demand growth of 4%-5%.\nMaarten Wormer, head of consulting at Aevean, told The Loadstar the apparent improvement in supply could be misleading.\n“That capacity figure flatters the picture, because most of it is the conflict-hit base recovering, not new supply,” he said.\nMeasured against 2025, Aevean expects demand to have grown around 10% by 2027, compared with capacity growth of 9%.\n“The market therefore ends 2027 slightly tighter than before the conflict, not looser,” Mr Wormer predicted.\nHe said combined capacity out of Doha, Dubai, and Abu Dhabi remained around 12% below pre-conflict levels, equivalent to approximately 2,200 tonnes a day.\nHowever, Gulf carriers Emirates, Qatar Airways, and Etihad were already operating at or above 2025 levels, with the shortfall almost entirely attributable to foreign freighter operators, whose capacity at the three hubs was down 49%.\n“If they do come back, they will mostly move freighters from other lanes rather than add new supply to the global market,” he said.\nTim van Leeuwen, VP and head of consulting at Rotate, agreed that the return of Gulf capacity was unlikely to have a substantial additional impact.\n“What is missing are mostly non-Gulf carriers operating to the region, but that missing capacity has been deployed elsewhere,” he told The Loadstar .\nHe said the consultant expected slightly weaker market conditions for airlines next year, although fuel-price uncertainty made rate predictions difficult.\nBoth consultancies expect considerable regional differences, with Asia-North America remaining constrained while Asia-Europe faces weaker demand and potentially excess capacity.\nAevean said Asia-North America air trade grew 24% in September and it expects demand on the corridor to increase by at least 10% in 2027. By contrast, Asia-Europe air trade fell 15% in September following the introduction of the EU’s €3 parcel duty, with ecommerce volumes down 40%, despite general cargo growing 7%.\nMr Wormer warned that returning Gulf capacity would be concentrated on the Asia-Europe corridor, potentially exacerbating the imbalance.\nRotate is already observing airlines shifting capacity from Asia-Europe towards Asia and North America in response to the weaker ecommerce demand.\nMr van Leeuwen said the Transpacific continued to face the greatest supply-demand pressure, with China-origin capacity particularly constrained and some US-bound cargo moving via Europe.\nSiddhant Iyer, head of cargo global partnerships and customer solutions at Cathay Cargo, also highlighted pressure on available space, with more customers turning to its Cathay Priority product to secure capacity on busy flights.\nHe said demand for specialist handling, including out-of-gauge server rack shipments, was increasing, while tonnage across Cathay’s special shipment solutions had risen 17% year on year.\nMeanwhile, Mr Wormer warned that AI-financing conditions and further export controls could alter the trajectory of technology-related cargo demand.\nAnother risk lies in the ageing aircraft fleet. Aevean estimates that around 1,600 freighters and widebody passenger aircraft are beyond typical retirement age, approximately 6.5 times average annual retirements.\n“If fuel stays high or demand softens, those aircraft are the first to be parked, and supply can fall faster than models assume,” Mr Wormer warned.\nThe consultant expects demand growth to begin outpacing capacity again in 2028, potentially tightening the market through to the end of the decade.",
